<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ghazi-ud-din Khan Imad-ul-Mulk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1736–1800</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ghazi-ud-din Khan Imad-ul-Mulk was a Mughal noble, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Asaf Jah I</a>, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> in 1753 and dominated the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. In 1754 he deposed and blinded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-bahadur">Ahmad Shah Bahadur</a> and set up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamgir-ii">Alamgir II</a>, whom he had murdered in 1759. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a>'s invasions he lost power and spent his later years as a fugitive, for a time under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> protection.</p></div>
